---
import DocsPage from "../../layouts/DocsPage.astro"

export const prerender = true
---

<DocsPage id="terminal-agent">

          <p>
            Running <code>vector</code> with no subcommand starts the interactive agent in the current directory, or in the path you pass. The published <code>vector</code> binary is free but needs a Vector account.
          </p>
          <p class="doc-cmd"><code>vector
vector ~/code/myapp --model anthropic/claude-sonnet-5</code><button class="doc-cmd-copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy this command">Copy</button></p>
          <p>
            It takes the same session flags as <code>run</code>: <code>--model</code>, <code>--agent</code>, <code>--prompt</code>, <code>--continue</code>, <code>--session</code> and <code>--fork</code>. <code>--mini</code> starts a minimal interface, and <code>--replay</code>, <code>--no-replay</code> and <code>--replay-limit</code> control how much history is redrawn when you resume or resize.
          </p>
          <p>
            For guarded personal free-model access in the upcoming 1.999.99 release, connect your free OpenRouter
            account with <code>vector providers login --provider openrouter --method "Connect OpenRouter"</code>,
            then use <code>vector models</code> to find eligible models. Use the saved provider connection or
            <code>OPENROUTER_API_KEY</code>; a project-only <code>provider.openrouter.options.apiKey</code> is insufficient.
            This personal setup works while Vector's shared allowance remains off. Keep the account unfunded and
            wait when its free limits are reached; the guarded route does not switch to a paid model or provider.
            See <a href="/docs/models#free-models">Models and providers</a> for the limited ZDR endpoint selection,
            account settings and current availability. It is not included in the currently published CLI 1.99.7.
          </p>

          <h2 id="install-the-cli">Install the CLI</h2>
          <p>Install the CLI from npm as <code>@vectordevai/cli</code>:</p>
          <p class="doc-cmd"><code>npm install -g @vectordevai/cli</code><button class="doc-cmd-copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy this command">Copy</button></p>
          <p>
            vectordev.ai serves a shell installer at <code>https://vectordev.ai/install</code> and a PowerShell installer at
            <code>https://vectordev.ai/install.ps1</code>. They download a standalone Vector CLI archive, check its size and
            SHA-256 checksum before installing, and do not require Node.js or npm. They install only standalone CLI releases
            that Vector has published for them, which are separate from the npm package; when the requested release is not
            published, the installer stops with an error and leaves any existing installation unchanged. No standalone CLI
            release is published for the installers yet, so today they stop with that error; install the CLI from npm
            or use the desktop download until then.
            <code>vector upgrade</code> and <code>vector uninstall</code> respect the verified installer or package manager
            that owns the running executable. On Windows, replacement of a running executable is scheduled after it exits;
            scheduled does not mean completed. Windows acceptance is still pending. Use the
            <a href="/releases">published release instructions</a> for available downloads.
          </p>

          <h2 id="cli-account">Account and CLI tokens</h2>
          <p>
            <code>vector login</code> opens the page that mints a token, or takes one directly with <code>--token</code>; <code>vector whoami</code> prints who is signed in and <code>vector logout</code> removes it. The same token is the <code>VECTOR_CLI_TOKEN</code> secret used by GitHub Actions.
          </p>
          <p class="doc-cmd"><code>vector login
vector whoami</code><button class="doc-cmd-copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy this command">Copy</button></p>
          <p class="note">
            The token is verified about once a day and cached with a seven-day offline grace window, so a flaky network never locks you out of your own terminal. Only a definitive invalid or expired answer signs you out. <code>login</code>, <code>logout</code>, <code>whoami</code>, <code>completion</code>, <code>upgrade</code> and <code>uninstall</code> work signed out.
          </p>

          <h2 id="cli-run">One-shot and scripted runs</h2>
          <p>
            <code>vector run</code> sends one prompt and prints the result. It is the surface you script against: <code>--format json</code> emits raw events for a pipeline, <code>--file</code> attaches files, <code>--model</code> and <code>--agent</code> choose who answers, <code>--variant</code> sets reasoning effort and <code>--title</code> names the session. <code>--continue</code> resumes the last session, <code>--session</code> a specific one, and <code>--fork</code> branches instead of appending.
          </p>
          <p class="doc-cmd"><code>vector run "why is the checkout test flaky?" --format json
vector run -c "now fix it" --variant high</code><button class="doc-cmd-copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy this command">Copy</button></p>

          <h2 id="cli-sessions">Sessions: list, export, import</h2>
          <p>
            <code>vector session list</code> prints recent sessions, with <code>--max-count</code> to cap them and <code>--format</code> for scripting; <code>vector session delete &lt;id&gt;</code> removes one permanently. Those ids are what <code>--session</code> takes elsewhere.
          </p>
          <p class="doc-cmd"><code>vector session list -n 20
vector export &lt;id&gt; --sanitize &gt; session.json
vector import session.json</code><button class="doc-cmd-copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy this command">Copy</button></p>
          <p>
            <code>vector export</code> writes a session's transcript and metadata as JSON, and <code>--sanitize</code> redacts the sensitive parts — transcript text, file contents, subtask commands — so it can be attached to a bug report. <code>vector import</code> reads a local JSON file back. Public session sharing and import from share URLs are no longer supported. <code>vector invite</code> remains available for live workspace access; see <a href="/docs/multiplayer">Multiplayer</a>.
          </p>

          <h2 id="cli-server">Server, web and editors</h2>
          <p>
            <code>vector serve</code> runs the agent as a headless server with <code>--port</code> and <code>--hostname</code>;
            <code>vector web</code> starts the same server and opens the browser interface. <code>vector attach &lt;url&gt;</code>
            connects a terminal session to a running server, with <code>--dir</code> to choose its directory and
            <code>--username</code> and <code>--password</code> for Basic authentication.
          </p>
          <p class="doc-cmd"><code>vector serve --port 4096
vector attach http://localhost:4096 --dir ~/code/myapp</code><button class="doc-cmd-copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy this command">Copy</button></p>
          <p>
            From 1.999.99, the next release, configure <code>VECTOR_SERVER_PASSWORD</code> and, optionally,
            <code>VECTOR_SERVER_USERNAME</code>; the default username is <code>vector</code>, and non-loopback binding requires
            a password unless you explicitly choose <code>--unsecured</code>. Releases before 1.999.99, such as desktop 1.99.8 and npm CLI 1.99.7, do not read these names or
            enforce that rule, so keep <code>vector serve</code> on loopback until you upgrade. Use a protected secret source
            for server passwords. From 1.999.99, the settings and database overrides are <code>VECTOR_AGENT_CONFIG</code>,
            <code>VECTOR_AGENT_CONFIG_DIR</code> and <code>VECTOR_AGENT_DB</code>.
            Keep the existing database file when migrating an ambiguous historical override.
          </p>
          <p>
            <code>vector acp</code> starts an Agent Client Protocol server for compatible editors. For programmatic HTTP
            use, see the <a href="/docs/sdk">SDK guide</a>; first public SDK publication remains pending.
          </p>
          <h3 id="wsl">Windows Subsystem for Linux</h3>
          <p>
            From 1.999.99, the next desktop release, Install Vector in server settings runs the shell installer with the exact
            CLI version that desktop requires, and that desktop release is published only after its required CLI version is
            available to the installer. It installs <code>~/.vector/bin/vector-native</code> using Linux download, archive and
            SHA-256 tools. Node.js and npm are not required. A matching installed engine can start without the download
            tools; a version mismatch requires an update.
          </p>
          <p>
            Sign in to the desktop before starting its managed WSL server. The desktop passes its Vector account token
            through the managed process's stdin and stops stale processes when the account changes or signs out. It does
            not put the token in command arguments, an installer receipt or a new credential file. Provider credentials
            remain separate; connect your chosen provider inside that environment when needed. Startup binds to Linux
            loopback, checks health and requires an unauthenticated request to be rejected before connecting. Actual
            Windows/WSL and published-installer validation remain release requirements.
          </p>
          <h3 id="public-transcripts-and-teams">Public transcripts and teams</h3>
          <p>
            Vector's source includes public transcript sharing on Vector's own service; it stays off until Vector
            completes its setup. Preview the content, explicitly choose
            publication and future updates, and set an expiry before sharing. Public links can expose visible prompts,
            code and tool output; importing creates passive local history. Removing a new Vector share does not remove a
            historical copy on another service. <code>vector invite</code> remains the separate live-workspace feature.
          </p>
          <p>
            Vector Teams offers <code>vector org list</code>, <code>vector org switch &lt;orgID&gt;</code> and
            <code>vector org personal</code>. A selected team supplies signed provider, permission and integration defaults;
            Personal clears selection even while offline. Refresh running apps after changing the selection from a separate
            CLI. Sharing and Teams require their production service setup before availability can be announced.
          </p>

          <h2 id="cli-maintenance">Upkeep, flags and your data</h2>
          <p>
            <code>vector upgrade</code> updates <code>@vectordevai/cli</code> to the newest npm version, or one you name. Supported <code>--method</code> values are <code>npm</code>, <code>pnpm</code> and <code>bun</code>. <code>vector uninstall</code> removes it, keeping configuration with <code>--keep-config</code> or sessions with <code>--keep-data</code>, and <code>--dry-run</code> lists what would go.
          </p>
          <p class="doc-cmd"><code>vector db path
vector db "select count(*) from session"</code><button class="doc-cmd-copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy this command">Copy</button></p>
          <p>
            Vector keeps sessions, messages and permissions in a local SQLite database. <code>vector db</code> opens a sqlite3 shell against it, <code>vector db "&lt;query&gt;"</code> runs one query with <code>--format</code> for the output shape, and <code>vector db path</code> prints where the file is. It is the plain answer to where your data lives: on your disk, in a file you can open yourself.
          </p>
          <ul class="feature-list">
            <li><strong>--print-logs</strong><span>Send logs to stderr.</span></li>
            <li><strong>--log-level</strong><span>DEBUG, INFO, WARN or ERROR.</span></li>
            <li><strong>--pure</strong><span>Run without external plugins.</span></li>
            <li><strong>vector completion</strong><span>Print a shell completion script.</span></li>
          </ul>
          <p class="note">
            Models, MCP servers, plugins and GitHub have terminal commands too: see
            <a href="/docs/models#cli-models">Models in the terminal</a>,
            <a href="/docs/mcp-and-plugins#cli-mcp">Custom MCP servers</a> and
            <a href="/docs/vectorscope#cli-github">GitHub from the terminal</a>.
          </p>
</DocsPage>
